# rule.require.specialize-a-rule-its-readers-look-past

> a rule whose every example draws from one domain will be read as a rule ABOUT that domain.

when a reader violates a general rule in a **second** domain, the repair is a **named specialization**, never a louder `.what`: a peer rule in the reader's domain, in its own words, with the general rule as parent. a split is the first move, never the last.

| when… | then… |
|---|---|
| you write a general rule with every example from one domain | split owed at authorship |
| a reader violates it in a domain it does not illustrate | specialize into their domain; do not bold the parent |
| you would bold, widen, or warn after a miss | did the reader ever recognize it applied? |
| you would add a second example | cheaper, weaker; weigh a named peer |

test: *in what situation would a reader who AGREES fail to notice it applies?* nameable → that is the specialization.

owes: specialization = claim in reader's vocabulary, own examples, pointer up · parent = pointer down + one line on why it split.

no fork per domain without evidence: reader looked past it → specialize · read it and disagreed → argue the rule · anticipated only → add an example.

blocker: a single-domain rule after a second-domain violation · repair by emphasis · a specialization with no parent pointer · a fork with no evidenced miss.
nitpick: a parent with no note of why it split.
